theory prod_size
imports nat, prod
description "The size of a product: the family the datatype layer generates for a datatype, stated here because `prod`'s own declaration has no arithmetic in scope."

-- The measure engine reads a datatype argument's size off the theory
-- (`fungen._size_family` looks up `<ty>_size` and its equations), and a
-- product's size is what a measure over a *tupled* argument needs:
-- `prod_size id id` measures `(nat, nat) prod`, so a recursion whose
-- argument is a tuple -- every mutual definition's encoded argument is --
-- has something to descend through.  The datatype layer would have
-- generated this family with the datatype (`datgen.expand_size`, Isabelle
-- declares `prod` where `+` is available), but `prod` is under `nat`
-- here: its declaration imports `logic_base`/`wf_base` only, so the
-- family is stated where the arithmetic is in scope instead.
--
-- The equation is the one `datgen.size_fun_lines` writes from
-- `measure.size_spec`: one for the constructor plus one summand per
-- measure parameter.  It has to be exactly that term -- the engine
-- unfolds a size application with `size_body` while the emitted proof
-- rewrites with this theorem, and the two are the same step only if the
-- right hand sides agree.

fun prod_size :: ('a ⇒ nat) ⇒ ('b ⇒ nat) ⇒ ('a,'b) prod ⇒ nat
  | prod_size f g (Pair a b) = 1 + f a + g b
